 were  against Jackson this was obviously one  way to direct opposition in the cradle  of anti masonry upstate New York there  was a very famous incident where  somebody William Morgan said he was  going to reveal Masonic secrets and then  was apparently  and killed and that led to charges of a  cover-up and this was in a context in  which I mean some historians have gone  back and showed that actually in a lot  of these communities there was heavy  overlap between people who are involved  with Freemasonry and people who were  involved with you know the higher  reaches of local societies in a  democracy are conspiracy theories more  active in the sense that you know we're  supposed to have open government or  transparent government and so are people  more worried about kind of dark shadowy  forces directing things I don't know if  it I think it's a different kind of  worry I mean obviously in monarchies  there's always the court intrigue you  know I mean and there's a long history  of you know the English being afraid of  Spanish conspiracies and vice versa you  know often there were really were spies  you know but I think that there's a  different flavor in a Democratic Society  what are some of the other conspiracy  theories kind of the the real classics  that you talk about in the book well one  of my favorites was the attempted  assassination of Andrew Jackson in 1835  Jackson himself believed that a senator  from Mississippi had arranged for or  attempted to arrange a hit on him  some of the projects and people jumped  on this at the same time auntie Jackson  people were claiming it was a false flag  attack that he had arranged for somebody  to fire at him but that's why the  pistols had failed was because he just  wanted public sympathy Lyndon Johnson  when the riots in the mid sixties were  hitting was convinced that the  Communists were behind it or strongly  suspected that the Communists were  behind it he asked his cabinet for  evidence to back this up the Attorney  General told him the evidence wasn't  there he was basically keep looking so I  mean it happens you know throughout  American history no part of the inside  of the book is that you know that  conspiracy theories as you were saying  or not fringe phenomena they're actually  at the center of American politics what  do we gain by looking at conspiracy  theories or if not taking the the  conspiracy seriously at least looking at  why do people keep returning to  conspiracy as a way of explaining things  well I think that even when a conspiracy  theory says absolutely nothing true  about  the object of the theory if it catches  on it says something true about the  anxieties and the experiences of the  people who believe it  if you look for example a when back in  the 1980s there is a spate of belief  that no white doctors were injecting  black babies with AIDS right I mean this  is not true right I mean I'm not gonna  claim it's true it's not but this comes  that you know and you can see how this  could catch on among people who've had  to deal with a long history of abusive  or high-handed treatment from white  doctors and why this sort of you would  sort of reach for something that  mythically expresses you know day to day  experience something similar also would  be a lot of new world order theories you  could see how people who feel like  they're losing control over their lives  would be attracted to ideas about a  cabal plotting against American liberty  and sovereignty and so on what is the  relevance of the book today what does  what does it offer a reader besides I  mean I an absolutely rollicking and  interesting and kind of you know  gimlet-eyed view of American history I  mean I think people will find it  fascinating for that but you know what  is its relevance to contemporary  American readers no I mean first I do  try to make it very entertaining so that  people could read it and enjoy it and  get a nothing ahead of it but I think  I'm trying to provide a tool kit I'm  trying to show these stories that keep  getting told again and again  I mean sometimes the same narratives  with different names plugged in and why  they've come up in the past and what  kind of in the circumstances they've  come up under so that when they turn up  again you won't say oh geez birthers  that's something that we've never heard  of before you know actually you know  there is precedent for this how did you  come to get interested in conspiracy  theory as a as a subject of inquiry well  I guess I in my teens I was reading  books about you know I mean actual sane  well-documented books about abuses by  the CIA and the FBI and so forth I mean  often drawing on the investigations that  were done in the 1970s and these were  often on the same shelves as somewhat  kookier books by you know people like  Fletcher Prouty you know making these  sort of stranger claims if I started  reading those and actually at first  thinking you know  this will be more you know more grist  for the mill and then I start saying you  know actually the level of evidence here  is not quite as good but it was  interesting and in its own right they  were interesting as stories and ran the  same time actually I discovered you know  some things like in the Church of the  SubGenius you know books by Robert Anton  Wilson and people who were in the book I  call them the the ironic style of  American paranoia people who are  interested in conspiracy theories not so  much as something to be espoused or  debunked but as just a sort of great  mutant mythos that you can have fun with  and just sort of build these great  stories and and just sort of find what  metaphors or laughs or insights or are  inside them and and and I in in some  ways I'm trying to extend that tradition  here I mean I'm looking at these stories  that people have told and I'm I'm not  making up a conspiracy theory of my own  but I'm saying what can what can we  unearth from these stories do you think 